      "metadata": {
        "id": "204a471902194f88"
      },
      "source": [
        "<div style=\"border-left: 5px solid #4B2E83; background-color: #F9F7FC; padding: 20px 25px; margin: 20px 0; border-radius: 5px;\">\n",
        "  <h2 style=\"color: #4B2E83; font-family: 'Encode Sans', 'Arial', sans-serif; margin-top: 0;\">Mandatory Elements of Every Visualization</h2>\n",
        "  <p style=\"font-size: 1.05em; color: #555; line-height: 1.7;\">A chart without context is just a picture. Every data visualization you produce — whether in a homework, a report, or a boardroom presentation — <strong>must</strong> include the following elements:</p>\n",
        "</div>\n",
        "\n",
        "| Element | Why it matters | Matplotlib method |\n",
        "|:--------|:---------------|:------------------|\n",
        "| **Title** | Tells the reader *what* the chart shows at a glance. A good title is specific: \"Survival Rate by Passenger Class\" beats \"Bar Chart\". | `ax.set_title()` |\n",
        "| **X-axis label** | Identifies the variable or categories on the horizontal axis. Without it the reader must guess what the ticks represent. | `ax.set_xlabel()` |\n",
        "| **Y-axis label** | Identifies the variable or scale on the vertical axis. Include units when applicable (e.g., \"Income (USD)\"). | `ax.set_ylabel()` |\n",
        "| **Legend** | When multiple series or groups share the same axes, the legend maps visual encodings (colour, marker, line style) to their meaning. | `ax.legend()` |\n",
        "| **Source** | Credits the data origin so the reader can verify claims or obtain the raw data. Typically placed as small text below the chart. | `fig.text()` or `ax.annotate()` |\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Rule of thumb:** If someone sees your chart for the first time — with no surrounding text — they should be able to understand *what* is plotted, *which variables* are shown, *which groups* are compared, and *where the data comes from*."
      ],
      "id": "204a471902194f88"
    },
