      },
      "source": [
        "<div style=\"border-left: 5px solid #4B2E83; background-color: #F9F7FC; padding: 20px 25px; margin: 20px 0; border-radius: 5px;\">\n",
        "  <h2 style=\"color: #4B2E83; font-family: 'Encode Sans', 'Arial', sans-serif; margin-top: 0;\">Why Plots Alone Are Not Enough</h2>\n",
        "  <p style=\"font-size: 1.05em; color: #555; line-height: 1.7;\">A beautiful chart is meaningless if the audience does not know <em>what the variables represent</em>, <em>how the data was collected</em>, or <em>what question it tries to answer</em>.</p>\n",
        "</div>\n",
        "\n",
        "Before creating a single plot you should:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Understand the domain** — What real-world process generated this data?\n",
        "2. **Read the data dictionary** — Know every column: its name, type, units, and allowed values.\n",
        "3. **Formulate questions** — Let curiosity drive exploration; the best insights come from well-posed questions.\n",
        "4. **Choose the right variables** — Match the variable type (categorical, ordinal, continuous) to the appropriate chart type.\n",
        "\n",
        "> A data dictionary is the *Rosetta Stone* of any dataset. Without it, column names like `SibSp` or `Parch` are gibberish."
      ],
      "id": "93990096ddf3c1ee"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "df15c713e2a800c6"
      },
      "source": [
        "<div style=\"border-left: 5px solid #4B2E83; background-color: #F9F7FC; padding: 20px 25px; margin: 20px 0; border-radius: 5px;\">\n",
        "  <h2 style=\"color: #4B2E83; font-family: 'Encode Sans', 'Arial', sans-serif; margin-top: 0;\">The Titanic Dataset — Historical Context</h2>\n",
        "</div>\n",
        "\n",
        "On **April 15, 1912**, the RMS Titanic — deemed \"unsinkable\" — struck an iceberg on her maiden voyage from Southampton to New York City and sank in the North Atlantic. Of the estimated **2,224 passengers and crew**, more than **1,500 perished**, making it one of the deadliest peacetime maritime disasters in history.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why study this data?**\n",
        "\n",
        "The Titanic disaster is far more than a historical curiosity. The survival patterns reveal deep social inequalities of the early 20th century: access to lifeboats was not random — it was shaped by class, gender, age, and ticket price. By analysing this dataset we can:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Quantify how socioeconomic status affected survival probability.\n",
        "- Examine the \"women and children first\" protocol with hard numbers.\n",
        "- Identify factors that modern safety regulations now address (lifeboat capacity, emergency procedures, class-based cabin locations).\n",
        "- Practice the full data-storytelling workflow on a rich, well-documented, and emotionally resonant dataset."
      ],
      "id": "df15c713e2a800c6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2bd1c4b5dfa02ebe"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Data Dictionary\n",
        "\n",
        "The Titanic dataset (Kaggle version, 891 rows) contains the following variables:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Variable | Type | Description |\n",
        "|:---------|:-----|:------------|\n",
        "| `PassengerId` | int | Unique identifier for each passenger |\n",
        "| `Survived` | int (0/1) | 0 = Did not survive, 1 = Survived |\n",
        "| `Pclass` | int (1/2/3) | Ticket class — proxy for socioeconomic status (1 = Upper, 2 = Middle, 3 = Lower) |\n",
        "| `Name` | string | Full name, including title (Mr, Mrs, Miss, etc.) |\n",
        "| `Sex` | string | `male` or `female` |\n",
        "| `Age` | float | Age in years (fractional for children < 1); has missing values |\n",
        "| `SibSp` | int | Number of siblings / spouses aboard |\n",
        "| `Parch` | int | Number of parents / children aboard |\n",
        "| `Ticket` | string | Ticket number |\n",
        "| `Fare` | float | Passenger fare in British Pounds |\n",
        "| `Cabin` | string | Cabin number (many missing values) |\n",
        "| `Embarked` | string | Port of embarkation: C = Cherbourg, Q = Queenstown, S = Southampton |"
      ],
      "id": "2bd1c4b5dfa02ebe"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "b19d133e1f88cbe"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Possible Research Questions\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Did passenger class significantly affect survival rates?** (Class privilege vs. equal treatment)\n",
        "2. **Was the \"women and children first\" protocol actually followed?** (Gender and age effects)\n",
        "3. **Did travelling with family help or hinder survival?** (SibSp, Parch)\n",
        "4. **Did the port of embarkation correlate with survival?** (If so, is it a proxy for class?)\n",
        "5. **How did fare (a continuous measure of wealth) relate to survival?**\n",
        "6. **What was the age distribution of survivors vs. non-survivors?**"
      ],
      "id": "b19d133e1f88cbe"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2003487c24111767"
      },
      "source": [
        "<div style=\"border-left: 5px solid #4B2E83; background-color: #F9F7FC; padding: 20px 25px; margin: 20px 0; border-radius: 5px;\">\n",
        "  <h2 style=\"color: #4B2E83; font-family: 'Encode Sans', 'Arial', sans-serif; margin-top: 0;\">Storytelling Plan</h2>\n",
        "  <p style=\"font-size: 1.05em; color: #555; line-height: 1.7;\"><em>Great data communication follows a narrative arc, not a bullet list of facts.</em></p>\n",
        "</div>\n",
        "\n",
        "The five principles below — inspired by the way Steven Levitt and Stephen Dubner structure *Freakonomics* — turn a pile of statistics into a story that sticks:\n",
        "\n",
        "| # | Principle | How we apply it to the Titanic |\n",
        "|:-:|:----------|:-------------------------------|\n",
        "| 1 | **Start with a question, not an answer** — \"Why do drug dealers live with their moms?\" hooks attention instantly. | *\"The Titanic had enough lifeboat capacity for only half its passengers. Who got a seat?\"* |\n",
        "| 2 | **Challenge assumptions with data** — the sumo & teacher cheating stories show data overturning \"common sense\". | Many assume survival was random or purely luck. The data shows **class, gender, and age** created a clear hierarchy. |\n",
