        "<div style=\"border-left: 5px solid #4B2E83; background-color: #F9F7FC; padding: 20px 25px; margin: 20px 0; border-radius: 5px;\">\n",
        "  <h2 style=\"color: #4B2E83; font-family: 'Encode Sans', 'Arial', sans-serif; margin-top: 0;\">3.2 Bar Plot with Error Bars</h2>\n",
        "</div>\n",
        "\n",
        "**What it is:** Same as a bar plot, but with vertical lines (error bars) that indicate the *uncertainty* around each estimate — typically the standard error or a confidence interval.\n",
        "\n",
        "**How to read it:** The bar shows the point estimate; the whiskers show how precise that estimate is. Non-overlapping error bars suggest a statistically meaningful difference.\n",
        "\n",
        "**When to use it:** Whenever you report a sample mean (or proportion) and want to convey how much it might vary due to sampling variability.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Titanic application:** Survival rate by class with 95 % confidence intervals."
      ],
      "id": "f4eeb9b66ef861ed"
    },
